The Scotland Act 1998, which was passed by the Parliament of the United Kingdom and given royal assent by Queen Elizabeth II on 19 November 1998, governs the functions and role of the Scottish Parliament and delimits its legislative competence. It places primary emphasis on protecting, enhancing and enhancing the powers, functions, competences and functions of the Scottish Parliament and the functions of other Scottish institutions. The Scotland Act 1998 imposes upon the Scottish Parliament the responsibility to provide a fair and efficient social and economic system and to enable Scotland to deliver its social and economic objectives.

The Scottish Parliament is responsible for:

the design of its own financial and budgetary policies;

the legislative and direct administration of the laws of Scotland;

the preparation of budget and expenditure documents;

the development of the welfare state and promoting the social objectives set out in the Scottish Social Attitudes Survey;

the promotion and co-ordination of the health service and the provision of health care;

the preparation of the national education service through the creation and implementation of a Scottish National curriculum for Scotland;

the establishment of schools;

the administration and maintenance of the education and the health systems;

environmental management and protection;

the regulation of Scottish markets and enterprises;

the administration and enforcement of national legislation.

The Scottish Parliament is empowered to adopt by-laws which supplement or provide additional provisions for the purpose of achieving any of its responsibilities, and its own enactments.

The Scotland Act 1998 sets out the general principles of the Scottish social security system and is enforced by the Department of Social Security.

The Scottish Government works in close partnership with Local Government, Local Enterprise Partnerships and Public Services Scotland to provide services through these organisations and other agencies and with other bodies that provide services related to social security.

