The Social Chapter is a chapter of the 1997 Treaty of Amsterdam covering social policy issues in European Union law. The term social policy refers to the policies and measures that are directed toward the advancement of social goods and the promotion of social equality and inclusion.

The Social Chapter has been designed to promote social progress, economic and social benefits and equality of development as well as social protection against the adverse impacts of economic and social developments. The Chapter is a key part of the European Union law that provides a framework for social policy implementation on an equal and non-discriminatory basis as regards Member States and, more generally, on trade relations of the Union.

The Social Chapter aims at achieving these objectives through:

(a) the regulation of economic and social developments and their effects on the population;

(b) social policies and welfare policies, including welfare measures, at national level, particularly policies of minimum social support;

(c) assistance to maintain or improve employment possibilities and conditions of employment at national level;

(d) public assistance for the improvement of the economic status of the population and for the alleviation of existing social problems;

(e) a social policy for the protection and promotion of the interests of the labour force by means of legislation directed at securing their fair share of economic benefits;

(f) measures to combat discrimination on grounds of sex, tradeunion activities and occupational or professional qualifications;

(g) measures to protect children from the harmful effects of information or cultural influences;

